## Relationship to Autodesk

groma is an independent, clean-room, read-only implementation. It is not
affiliated with, endorsed by, or derived from Autodesk, Inc.

"Autodesk", "Revit", "RVT" and "BIM 360" are trademarks or registered
trademarks of Autodesk, Inc. They are used here only nominatively — to state
which file formats this software reads — and not as branding for this project:
the name "groma" is a Roman surveying instrument and refers to no product of
Autodesk's. Where an earlier name of this project survives, it survives inside
a file format rather than on it — the eight bytes a packed scene begins with,
documented in `crates/scene-pack` and kept because scenes already written
carry them.

groma contains no Autodesk source code, no decompiled Autodesk binaries, no
Autodesk SDK, and no code derived from the Revit API or the ODA BimRv SDK. The
format knowledge in `docs/` was obtained by observing bytes in files the
project's authors were entitled to read, and every recovered rule is recorded
with the measurement that justifies it.

The IFC side reads and writes ISO 10303-21 and IFC4, which are open published
standards; nothing in this paragraph is needed for that half of the work.

Two boundaries are deliberate and must be preserved by contributors:

1. **No proprietary model content is redistributed.** `.rvt`, `.rfa`, `.rte`
   and `.rft` files are ignored by git, and `fixtures/README.md` sets the rule
   for anything that is committed: only samples with explicit redistribution
   permission, recorded with provenance, license, Revit release and SHA-256.
   The test suite builds synthetic CFB files at runtime instead.

2. **No circumvention of protection measures.** Reading and describing a file
   format is interoperability work. Defeating a technical protection measure is
   a different legal category. In particular, the high-entropy block found in
   the inter-member gaps is deliberately left undecoded, and this project does
   not accept contributions that decode it.

Reverse engineering for interoperability is protected in the jurisdictions this
project is developed in — see EU Directive 2009/24/EC arts. 5(3) and 6, DMCA
§1201(f), *Sega v. Accolade* and *Sony v. Connectix* — but that protection
depends on staying inside the two boundaries above.


## Third-party components

groma's dependencies are declared in `Cargo.toml` and pinned in `Cargo.lock`;
each carries its own license. Run `cargo tree` to enumerate them.

The viewer page ships as a built bundle, `web/viewer-ui.js`, so unlike the Rust
dependencies these are redistributed here in object form and their notices have
to travel with them. It is built by `npm run build:web` from
`web/src/viewer-ui.jsx` and contains, under the MIT license:

- React, React DOM 19.3.0 and scheduler 0.28.0 - Copyright (c) Meta Platforms,
  Inc. and affiliates. <https://github.com/facebook/react/blob/main/LICENSE>
- anime.js 4.5.0 - Copyright (c) 2025 Julian Garnier.
  <https://github.com/juliangarnier/anime/blob/master/LICENSE.md>

The MIT license grants use, copying, modification and distribution provided its
notice is kept with the software; the bundle carries that notice in its own
banner, and the versions are pinned in `package.json` and `package-lock.json`.
Combining them into an AGPL work is permitted - MIT imposes no reciprocal
condition - and the bundle as a whole is offered under the AGPL.

The Revit 2023 and 2026 catalogue tables under `crates/revit-catalog/src/` are
built from publicly documented enumeration names and identifiers. They contain
no Autodesk source code. `scripts/generate_revit_catalog.py` records how they
were produced.
